WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.886
Safe Rust uses lifetimes to enforce
reference validity,

2
00:00:02.966 --> 00:00:06.099
but unsafe code and FFI boundaries can
move some responsibility back to the

3
00:00:06.179 --> 00:00:06.725
programmer.

4
00:00:06.805 --> 00:00:09.888
Raw pointers do not carry the same checked
lifetime relationships as safe

5
00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:10.515
references.

6
00:00:10.595 --> 00:00:13.379
External code may follow rules Rust cannot
verify automatically.

7
00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:16.790
FFI boundaries often involve allocation,
deallocation, buffers, callbacks,

8
00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:17.765
and thread behavior.

9
00:00:17.845 --> 00:00:18.690
Who owns the memory?

10
00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:19.416
Who may free it?

11
00:00:19.496 --> 00:00:20.988
How long must a buffer remain valid?

12
00:00:21.068 --> 00:00:23.404
Can external code retain a pointer after a
call returns?

13
00:00:23.484 --> 00:00:25.672
Can a callback run after the Rust owner
has gone away?

14
00:00:25.752 --> 00:00:29.033
These questions are lifetime questions
even when no Rust lifetime annotation

15
00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:29.511
appears.

16
00:00:29.591 --> 00:00:33.022
A safe wrapper around unsafe or external
code must preserve the lifetime contract

17
00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:33.847
for safe callers.

18
00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:37.010
The wrapper should prevent ordinary safe
Rust code from creating invalid

19
00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:37.786
relationships.

20
00:00:37.866 --> 00:00:40.252
If the wrapper cannot enforce everything
through types,

21
00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:42.669
it should document the remaining
assumptions clearly.

22
00:00:42.749 --> 00:00:45.831
Safety contracts should explain what data
must remain valid, who owns it,

23
00:00:45.911 --> 00:00:48.447
who may free it, how long references or
callbacks may be used,

24
00:00:48.527 --> 00:00:50.167
and what the caller is responsible for.

25
00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:52.534
A comment that simply says a block is safe
is not enough.

26
00:00:52.614 --> 00:00:56.144
The evidence needs to connect the unsafe
operation to the lifetime assumptions that

27
00:00:56.224 --> 00:00:56.821
make it valid.

28
00:00:56.901 --> 00:01:00.282
A good review also asks whether the safe
API prevents misuse by ordinary callers.

29
00:01:00.362 --> 00:01:03.693
If a caller can pass a temporary buffer to
external code that stores it for later,

30
00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:06.010
the wrapper has not protected the lifetime
contract.

31
00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.526
If callback registration can outlive the
captured state,

32
00:01:08.606 --> 00:01:10.992
the API needs a clearer ownership or
cancellation model.

33
00:01:11.072 --> 00:01:14.503
Testing and review are important because
compiler enforcement is weaker at these

34
00:01:14.583 --> 00:01:15.130
boundaries.

35
00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.093
Reviewers should focus on ownership
transfer, allocation pairing,

36
00:01:18.173 --> 00:01:21.156
callback lifetime, buffer validity,
synchronization, and API shape.

37
00:01:21.236 --> 00:01:23.573
The best unsafe boundary is small,
documented, tested,

38
00:01:23.653 --> 00:01:25.940
and wrapped in a safe interface that
protects callers.
